(Course summary) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Gradient descent is an optimisation algorithm that minimises a loss function by repeatedly) Tj T*
(moving the parameters in the direction of the negative gradient.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The learning rate controls the step size. If the learning rate is too large the loss can) Tj T*
(diverge or oscillate; if it is too small, training converges very slowly.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Stochastic gradient descent \(SGD\) estimates the gradient from one example or a mini-batch,) Tj T*
(which makes each step cheaper but noisier. Momentum averages past gradients to smooth the) Tj T*
(updates.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A model with high bias underfits: it is too simple and has high training error.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A model with high variance overfits: it has low training error but high test error.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Regularisation such as L2 weight decay or dropout reduces variance by penalising complex) Tj T*
(models. Cross-validation estimates generalisation error.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Precision is the fraction of predicted positives that are truly positive.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Recall is the fraction of actual positives that the model finds.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall. On imbalanced data accuracy can) Tj T*
(be misleading, because always predicting the majority class gives high accuracy.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
